# Testing

## Naming Convention

- Unit test method names MUST follow:
  `[methodName]_[stateUnderTest]_[expectedBehavior]`
- Example: `mapCellInfoNr_withValidPci_returnsCorrectDomainModel`.

## Mapper Coverage

- Every mapper that converts a raw Android framework class (`CellInfoLte`,
  `CellInfoNr`, `PhysicalChannelConfig`, etc.) into an internal domain model
  MUST have isolated unit tests. This is not optional.
- Tests must construct/mock the framework input directly — never depend on
  a real device or emulator radio state.

## Testing Stack

- JUnit5 (Jupiter) for test execution, run via the `de.mannodermaus.android-junit`
  Gradle plugin (2.0.1+; the project behind it is now named
  `android-junit-framework` — it was previously published as
  `de.mannodermaus.android-junit5`/`android-junit5`, so don't search for that
  old name).
- This plugin supports both JUnit 5 and JUnit 6, but JUnit 6 requires API 35+
  on the device. Stick with JUnit 5 (Jupiter) until minSdk is raised above 29
  — do not adopt JUnit 6 before then.
- MockK for mocking framework dependencies (`CellInfo` subtypes,
  `TelephonyManager`, `PhysicalChannelConfig`, etc.).
- Turbine for testing Flow emissions (not `kotlinx-coroutines-test`).

## Modem Edge Cases

- Tests must cover modem edge cases, including sentinel/unavailable values
  such as `Int.MAX_VALUE` returned for a radio parameter (e.g. RSRP,
  EARFCN) when the value is unavailable — assert these map to `null` (or the
  documented "unknown" representation), not the raw sentinel.
